Five distinct changes in one commit was a scope call — I could
have sent them as a patch series, but they all stem from the same run and
correcting them separately would fragment the "lessons learned" narrative.
The `origin/master` -> `origin/HEAD` sweep is the most mechanical of the
five; if it needs reverting independently, it's easy to extract.

The "Trust the loop" note references 247 tags running in seconds. That
number came from the actual run described in the issues file, so it's
grounded — but it may age badly if someone reads this on a much slower
machine with thousands of tags. I left it in because the concrete number
is more persuasive than "it's fast, trust us".
